1
00:00:00,431 --> 00:00:21,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, on the topic of non-conflict, which is one of my favorite topics, I found a quote from Ajahn Chah, in what is called the Korwat manual, from Wat Panna Nachat or Wat Papam.

2
00:00:22,797 --> 00:00:27,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Korwat is the monastery etiquette or the house rules of a particular monastery.

3
00:00:28,637 --> 00:00:32,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, vinaya is just the particular monastery's house rules.

4
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:39,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for example, your mother says, you know, don't walk inside the house with your shoes.

5
00:00:39,962 --> 00:00:40,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

6
00:00:41,443 --> 00:00:42,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't do that.

7
00:00:44,804 --> 00:00:53,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And, so, this manual is just... tells you how to live within that monastery.

8
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:04,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there could... there's a number of rules, but again, they don't come from the Buddha, per se.

9
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:21,730
[SPEAKER_00]: The book... well, sorry, the kawat, or vinaya, is usually used as a weapon, you know, to catch people out.

10
00:01:22,424 --> 00:01:25,525
[SPEAKER_00]: To prove that you're right, other people argue.

11
00:01:27,945 --> 00:01:29,426
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not used in the right way.

12
00:01:31,426 --> 00:01:35,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this book begins with this Ajahn Chah quote.

13
00:01:37,807 --> 00:01:43,329
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, It's not right to watch others with a fault-finding mind.

14
00:01:45,189 --> 00:01:46,109
[SPEAKER_00]: This won't help you.

15
00:01:47,490 --> 00:01:49,290
[SPEAKER_00]: This won't help your practice at all.

16
00:01:50,906 --> 00:01:55,188
[SPEAKER_00]: If you feel annoyed, observe that annoyance in your heart.

17
00:01:56,788 --> 00:02:06,052
[SPEAKER_00]: If other people's sīla is not perfect, or they do not behave like good monks, good people, it is not your job to be judgmental.

18
00:02:08,152 --> 00:02:12,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't become wise by watching and blaming others.

19
00:02:12,454 --> 00:02:17,336
[SPEAKER_00]: The vinaya is a tool to assist you in developing samādhi.

20
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:23,230
[SPEAKER_00]: It is not a weapon for finding fault or judging who's good or who's bad.

21
00:02:24,451 --> 00:02:25,792
[SPEAKER_00]: No one can practice for you.

22
00:02:27,093 --> 00:02:28,594
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't practice for anyone else.

23
00:02:29,454 --> 00:02:31,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, be mindful of your own behavior.

24
00:02:31,856 --> 00:02:33,197
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the path of practice.

25
00:02:34,598 --> 00:02:37,260
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the Buddhist type of Bible bashing.

26
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:37,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

27
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:39,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

28
00:02:39,661 --> 00:02:40,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just...

29
00:02:40,582 --> 00:02:41,222
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, that's the point.

30
00:02:41,242 --> 00:02:43,724
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why, like, why is the fault finding?

31
00:02:45,645 --> 00:02:46,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it bad?

32
00:02:49,736 --> 00:02:56,781
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to anything, not specifically viñña or something, but even what somebody says something, and they're wrong.

33
00:02:57,062 --> 00:02:58,563
[SPEAKER_01]: They might be completely wrong in what they said.

34
00:02:59,043 --> 00:03:01,685
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is you having a mind of looking for faults?

35
00:03:01,946 --> 00:03:03,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that wrong for you?

36
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:08,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's got nothing to do with you.

37
00:03:10,092 --> 00:03:13,015
[SPEAKER_00]: And the important part is, where are you wrong?

38
00:03:14,403 --> 00:03:15,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

39
00:03:15,224 --> 00:03:16,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is your fault?

40
00:03:16,345 --> 00:03:17,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Well... What is your mind?

41
00:03:17,786 --> 00:03:18,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, that's... No.

42
00:03:19,228 --> 00:03:22,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but why is looking for faults... Why is that a fault?

43
00:03:24,073 --> 00:03:25,955
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you're looking for fault, you're already at fault.

44
00:03:26,535 --> 00:03:29,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you think you aren't at fault.

45
00:03:30,159 --> 00:03:31,460
[SPEAKER_01]: That's kind of implicit, yeah?

46
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:31,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

47
00:03:32,521 --> 00:03:34,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you think you... you're right.

48
00:03:34,123 --> 00:03:36,005
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is it... why... why... why another reason?

49
00:03:36,025 --> 00:03:38,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would... why would it be also fault?

50
00:03:44,255 --> 00:03:48,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's like what we addressed in the previous... Well, yes, exactly, yeah.

51
00:03:48,736 --> 00:03:49,896
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes from that framework.

52
00:03:50,536 --> 00:03:53,077
[SPEAKER_01]: A predetermined framework of your mind.

53
00:03:53,837 --> 00:03:56,998
[SPEAKER_01]: You're looking for faults means you already have a reason to look for faults.

54
00:03:57,018 --> 00:03:58,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Means it doesn't matter what that person says.

55
00:03:59,298 --> 00:04:00,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already looking for faults.

56
00:04:01,458 --> 00:04:05,019
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot look for faults on account of understanding what they said.

57
00:04:05,359 --> 00:04:08,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You can agree or disagree with what they said because you understood it.

58
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:13,901
[SPEAKER_01]: But the mind of looking for faults actually puts whatever they said secondary.

59
00:04:15,443 --> 00:04:18,025
[SPEAKER_01]: And prioritize that it's all like irrational approach.

60
00:04:18,426 --> 00:04:19,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Approach of...

61
00:04:19,907 --> 00:04:25,872
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm looking for faults now, which means whatever it's going to be said, it's going to be seen against the context of finding faults.

62
00:04:26,272 --> 00:04:26,873
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said.

63
00:04:26,893 --> 00:04:32,657
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one of the main reasons that people will be unable to develop the right view, even when they come across the right teaching.

64
00:04:33,578 --> 00:04:35,460
[SPEAKER_01]: They have the mind of fault-finding.

65
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:41,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they will not understand that which they would have otherwise understood and become a Sotāpanna.

66
00:04:43,045 --> 00:04:46,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that doesn't mean you, like, blanket, accept everything that's said.

67
00:04:46,808 --> 00:04:52,493
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you lose your, like, discriminative ability and discernment.

68
00:04:53,014 --> 00:05:00,761
[SPEAKER_01]: That means you don't give in to the attitude of looking for faults, which is, as I said, independent of the content that's being described or conveyed to you.

69
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:01,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've decided beforehand.

70
00:05:01,782 --> 00:05:02,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much, yeah.

71
00:05:02,522 --> 00:05:04,004
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a fault, and I'm going to point to that.

72
00:05:04,044 --> 00:05:09,869
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have an inclination to look for fault, and simply ignoring that inclination means that your mind stayed.

73
00:05:10,249 --> 00:05:19,361
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you might have an inclination to resist to how you feel, resist towards the disagreeable, and if you ignore that, means you're giving in to your will.

74
00:05:20,462 --> 00:05:29,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to develop enough self-awareness to be able to not maintain the mind of looking for faults, even amidst things that you find disagreeable.

75
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:43,799
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if that disagreeability is not rooted in you looking for fault, but it's rooted in you understanding the meaning, and understanding the contradiction in the meaning, and understanding why that meaning is wrong, means that's absolutely fine.

76
00:05:43,899 --> 00:05:47,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You can now dismiss that thing, because you understood it was wrong.

77
00:05:47,915 --> 00:05:49,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just like... and you will know that.

78
00:05:49,796 --> 00:06:05,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you just hear something said and you say that's wrong, but you have no argument as to why it's wrong, and you start to, like, scrape left and right irrationally to justify why that's wrong, means you're already looking for faults and you're already chosen to not to accept that, even if you don't know really what it means.

79
00:06:07,406 --> 00:06:10,949
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... if you're accepting something, you want to know why you're accepting it.

80
00:06:11,789 --> 00:06:13,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Why it makes sense to you.

81
00:06:13,926 --> 00:06:17,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you find this such-and-such statement or teaching agreeable?

82
00:06:18,569 --> 00:06:22,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And say, if you're not accepting it, you want to know why you are not accepting it.

83
00:06:22,471 --> 00:06:23,391
[SPEAKER_01]: What is wrong with it?

84
00:06:27,913 --> 00:06:32,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you say, oh, this contradicts such-and-such, you need to know why and how to what extent.

85
00:06:32,256 --> 00:06:39,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just throw that, oh, this is just contradiction, but have no means of supporting it, or have extremely irrational means of supporting it.

86
00:06:40,794 --> 00:06:45,116
[SPEAKER_01]: It all points out that, oh, you already decided you don't want to accept this, now you just need to justify why.

87
00:06:46,177 --> 00:06:48,919
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be to your own demerit, basically.

88
00:06:49,439 --> 00:06:53,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might come across things that are very accurate, very right, and would have freed you from suffering.

89
00:06:53,961 --> 00:07:02,606
[SPEAKER_01]: But because of your fault-finding mind, as I would have said, you would have not actually landed there and gained the understanding of it, because you dismissed it beforehand.

90
00:07:02,626 --> 00:07:09,070
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, if you're honest with yourself, you would ask, I don't know what the truth is, but which is more likely?

91
00:07:10,927 --> 00:07:13,770
[SPEAKER_01]: If I were to hear the truth, which is more likely?

92
00:07:13,790 --> 00:07:15,552
[SPEAKER_01]: To find it agreeable or disagreeable?

93
00:07:16,454 --> 00:07:18,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Just off the top of your head, what would be the first answer?

94
00:07:18,956 --> 00:07:20,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not going to be what I...

95
00:07:20,418 --> 00:07:25,284
[SPEAKER_01]: It's of course going to be against my whole life, against the whole nature of being and existence.

96
00:07:25,304 --> 00:07:27,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma goes against the stream, against the grain.

97
00:07:28,848 --> 00:07:31,271
[SPEAKER_01]: How's that going to be felt, if you always went with the grain?

98
00:07:32,945 --> 00:07:45,478
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, already you can, even as a putujana, who is not, you know, not a monk, not practicing fully committedly, you can actually still not forsake a good line of reasoning.

99
00:07:45,498 --> 00:07:47,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so it's more likely to be upsetting.

100
00:07:48,321 --> 00:07:49,863
[SPEAKER_01]: So now you find things that upset you.

101
00:07:50,765 --> 00:07:53,007
[SPEAKER_01]: teachings that make you displeased.

102
00:07:53,688 --> 00:07:55,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Now understand why is that.

103
00:07:56,030 --> 00:07:58,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because, oh, well, because the way he speaks.

104
00:07:58,432 --> 00:07:59,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's not an argument.

105
00:07:59,533 --> 00:08:00,854
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not a valid argument.

106
00:08:01,495 --> 00:08:04,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'll dismiss what that person says because of how they said it.

107
00:08:05,359 --> 00:08:06,400
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's an excuse.

108
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:07,761
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not an argument.

109
00:08:08,791 --> 00:08:17,819
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to dismiss what this person said, or if you want to accept what that person said, you need to understand why, and not just say, oh, because it displeases me or pleases me, respectively.

110
00:08:18,299 --> 00:08:20,521
[SPEAKER_01]: That's irrational basis for your understanding.

111
00:08:20,541 --> 00:08:21,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no understanding.

112
00:08:23,043 --> 00:08:25,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you accept something, you want to know why.

113
00:08:26,326 --> 00:08:27,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Why does this make sense to me?

114
00:08:28,808 --> 00:08:31,591
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't want to accept it, you want to know why this doesn't make sense to me.

115
00:08:31,932 --> 00:08:37,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you find the answer why it doesn't make sense to you, then question that answer.

116
00:08:37,919 --> 00:08:39,441
[SPEAKER_01]: See whether that answer makes sense.

117
00:08:40,081 --> 00:08:42,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you just jumped on the answer because it pleased you.

118
00:08:42,845 --> 00:08:47,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it offered you like a leeway to step back and ignore what you heard and it was displeasing to you.

119
00:08:51,647 --> 00:08:53,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you already start going against the grain.

120
00:08:54,068 --> 00:08:59,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Against the grain of accepting things at the face value, which is a definition of human existence, pretty much.

121
00:09:04,313 --> 00:09:08,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to go back to Ajahn Chah's quotes a little bit.

122
00:09:09,956 --> 00:09:19,400
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if another person's sīla is not perfect, or they do not behave like good monks,

123
00:09:20,372 --> 00:09:21,573
[SPEAKER_01]: But your idea of a good monk is?

124
00:09:21,873 --> 00:09:23,073
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your problem, basically.

125
00:09:23,133 --> 00:09:24,734
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not your job to be judgmental.

126
00:09:25,514 --> 00:09:26,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not your problem.

127
00:09:27,455 --> 00:09:27,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

128
00:09:27,775 --> 00:09:32,497
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said previously, that doesn't mean you will be blind to the behavior.

129
00:09:33,218 --> 00:09:36,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you dislike it, you will maybe start looking into why you're disliking it.

130
00:09:37,099 --> 00:09:38,940
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to, no, we must police that.

131
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:41,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially when there's nothing wrong.

132
00:09:41,161 --> 00:09:43,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, because that's the whole point of that kovat, is it's not the vinaya.

133
00:09:44,462 --> 00:09:49,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's nothing immoral in regard to not walking with shoes in your house.

134
00:09:49,185 --> 00:09:50,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's immoral.

135
00:09:50,673 --> 00:09:51,873
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing immoral about it.

136
00:09:52,953 --> 00:09:56,334
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why good covert is the covert that has a practical application, basis.

137
00:09:56,814 --> 00:10:02,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Bad covert is just whimsical sort of covert made by the people in the position of power over junior ones.

138
00:10:02,556 --> 00:10:03,576
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's beside the point.

139
00:10:04,296 --> 00:10:08,137
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is to recognize whether you agree with the covert or not, and why is that?

140
00:10:08,177 --> 00:10:09,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you mind that?

141
00:10:09,217 --> 00:10:18,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if somebody done it for wrong reasons, simply to entertain their sense of control over others, they create covert rules to see if everybody will obey, why do you have a problem obeying that?

142
00:10:19,586 --> 00:10:20,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because you're conceited?

143
00:10:21,207 --> 00:10:22,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it your problem?

144
00:10:22,527 --> 00:10:25,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, it's not your problem to find fault in others.

145
00:10:26,249 --> 00:10:29,010
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, it's not your problem to be affected by others' faults.

146
00:10:29,671 --> 00:10:31,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Or people finding fault in you.

147
00:10:31,251 --> 00:10:33,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Or people finding... Why does that upset me?

148
00:10:33,432 --> 00:10:34,493
[SPEAKER_01]: People finding fault in me.

149
00:10:34,613 --> 00:10:35,353
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what people do.

150
00:10:35,994 --> 00:10:37,354
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot possibly control that.

151
00:10:39,715 --> 00:10:42,957
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, the power of the fools, of ignorant minds, is to complain.

152
00:10:44,256 --> 00:10:45,577
[SPEAKER_01]: They complain if you speak fast.

153
00:10:45,637 --> 00:10:48,219
[SPEAKER_01]: They complain if you speak too low, too slow.

154
00:10:48,419 --> 00:10:49,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people will.

155
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:51,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people say, I don't like because he speaks too loud.

156
00:10:51,741 --> 00:10:54,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people say, I don't like because he speaks too quiet, not loud enough.

157
00:10:54,804 --> 00:10:57,466
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always going to be something that people will complain about.

158
00:10:58,306 --> 00:10:59,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that my problem?

159
00:10:59,687 --> 00:11:00,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Other people's complaints.

160
00:11:01,188 --> 00:11:04,250
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you already have that, that doesn't mean I don't care what other people say.

161
00:11:04,391 --> 00:11:09,354
[SPEAKER_01]: It means I don't make a problem out of it, which means I will not lose the context in regard to what they're saying.

162
00:11:09,454 --> 00:11:12,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you might... Oh, yeah, maybe I am speaking too loud or

163
00:11:13,805 --> 00:11:15,486
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the purpose of my speaking?

164
00:11:15,546 --> 00:11:16,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's to convey the message.

165
00:11:18,527 --> 00:11:23,370
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is the better way to convey the message and fulfill the purpose of my speaking?

166
00:11:24,290 --> 00:11:25,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, maybe I should speak slowly.

167
00:11:25,851 --> 00:11:27,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, oh, it doesn't really matter how I speak.

168
00:11:27,832 --> 00:11:28,752
[SPEAKER_01]: People can slow it down.

169
00:11:29,553 --> 00:11:32,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, then I won't deal with it, because it's not my problem.

170
00:11:35,296 --> 00:11:41,599
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you are affected by other people's complaints, you will not have that context and that perspective to review your own behavior.

171
00:11:42,456 --> 00:11:45,000
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, oh, actually, no, I was simply careless about it.

172
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:48,064
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a big deal to me, but it's a big deal to everybody else.

173
00:11:48,645 --> 00:11:49,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I could change that.

174
00:11:49,686 --> 00:11:50,087
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mind.

175
00:11:50,107 --> 00:11:50,868
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not conceited.

176
00:11:50,928 --> 00:11:51,549
[SPEAKER_01]: I must hold it.

177
00:11:51,969 --> 00:11:57,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, again, that's like in the suttas when, like, a vast majority of people complain about something completely insignificant.

178
00:11:57,677 --> 00:11:59,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It has nothing to do with practice or anything.

179
00:12:00,545 --> 00:12:02,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha said, okay, you stop doing it.

180
00:12:03,007 --> 00:12:05,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And you go, but why did the Buddha give in to these complaints?

181
00:12:05,149 --> 00:12:05,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You should draw a line.

182
00:12:06,450 --> 00:12:09,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, you could draw a line, but that would make people complain more.

183
00:12:10,014 --> 00:12:11,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means now you have to deal with that.

184
00:12:11,555 --> 00:12:15,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you deal with that, you won't be able to deal with your own practice and seclusion and being left alone.

185
00:12:16,459 --> 00:12:23,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, if you meet the requirement, ignorant requirement, of the fools that complained, you'll be left alone.

186
00:12:24,667 --> 00:12:24,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

187
00:12:25,794 --> 00:12:28,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you still have work to do, you want to be left alone.

188
00:12:28,616 --> 00:12:34,820
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't have a work to do, if you completed your work in terms of dhamma, yeah, deal with complaints, talk back, whatever you want to do.

189
00:12:36,902 --> 00:12:42,046
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you still recognize there's work to be done and you have to be careful to protect the environment that will support that work.

190
00:12:42,966 --> 00:12:47,770
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's not like you don't care about people complaining means I'll dismiss everybody or I'll fight back.

191
00:12:48,450 --> 00:12:51,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It simply means I won't lose perspective on what's important to me.

192
00:12:52,053 --> 00:12:52,373
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

193
00:12:54,505 --> 00:12:56,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And, by the way, that applies to lay people as well, not just monks.

194
00:12:57,386 --> 00:12:59,407
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's like one of the main characteristics in the world.

195
00:12:59,827 --> 00:13:01,248
[SPEAKER_01]: What will other people say about me?

196
00:13:01,608 --> 00:13:02,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And you live your whole life governed by it.

197
00:13:03,048 --> 00:13:07,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you have people who kind of try to dismiss that and say, I don't care what others say, and then go to the other extreme.

198
00:13:08,731 --> 00:13:11,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, you can try and deny caring what others say.

199
00:13:11,792 --> 00:13:14,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you hear what others say, which means it affects you to some extent.

200
00:13:16,163 --> 00:13:19,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about complying blindly nor resisting blindly.

201
00:13:20,005 --> 00:13:25,349
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about not being affected in the first place and then it doesn't matter whether you comply or you have a reason for resisting.

202
00:13:25,969 --> 00:13:28,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sometimes the Buddha had reasons and resisted them.

203
00:13:28,091 --> 00:13:30,532
[SPEAKER_01]: The vast majority of people saying monks should be doing this.

204
00:13:30,572 --> 00:13:31,273
[SPEAKER_01]: He said no.

205
00:13:32,473 --> 00:13:34,635
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't always you go along with the majority.

206
00:13:35,616 --> 00:13:38,077
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically, it's determined by the context at the time.

207
00:13:41,619 --> 00:13:42,460
[SPEAKER_01]: When all those, eh...

208
00:13:44,665 --> 00:13:48,729
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a group of those monks who were kind of living in dependence of their whole area.

209
00:13:49,089 --> 00:13:54,274
[SPEAKER_01]: It was mentioned in the suttas, and they would... Well, basically, they were like householders in robes.

210
00:13:54,874 --> 00:14:02,241
[SPEAKER_01]: They would go and spend time with lay people, with their homes, talking about their lay affairs, laughing with them, joking with them, associating with them.

211
00:14:03,202 --> 00:14:05,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, oh, come here, offer this, offer that.

212
00:14:05,444 --> 00:14:09,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, just the whole, like, living basically like a kind of full-on part of that

213
00:14:10,527 --> 00:14:11,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Lay society.

214
00:14:11,868 --> 00:14:19,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the monk who was actually bent on practicing the dhamma came to the village and went in the path and he wasn't looking at people, he wasn't talking to them, he wasn't smiling at them.

215
00:14:19,953 --> 00:14:23,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And all this province got upset, saying, look at this conceited monk.

216
00:14:24,556 --> 00:14:31,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Our monks, they talk to us, they're friendly with us, they listen to our advice, they, you know, what we say they should do, they do along with it.

217
00:14:32,721 --> 00:14:34,222
[SPEAKER_01]: But this monk doesn't even look at us.

218
00:14:34,782 --> 00:14:36,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, he's conceited, he's this, he's that.

219
00:14:37,584 --> 00:14:38,865
[SPEAKER_01]: And they refuse to give him the food.

220
00:14:40,136 --> 00:14:45,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would think now, if you were to follow majority rule, okay, so in that province, we all must behave like this.

221
00:14:45,519 --> 00:14:45,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

222
00:14:46,359 --> 00:14:56,644
[SPEAKER_01]: What the Buddha did, he said to Sariputta, collect a greater amount of monks, more than the monks that lived there, and go there and behave like that monk that was quiet.

223
00:14:57,467 --> 00:15:08,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And when this vast majority of monks came, behaving quietly, not looking, not smiling, not chatting with them, then they realize, oh, crap, actually, it was the monks that we know, were... they're the ones behaving differently.

224
00:15:08,672 --> 00:15:09,872
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how the monks should behave.

225
00:15:10,352 --> 00:15:15,434
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha corrected the lay people's expectations and views, which in a way, was nothing unreasonable.

226
00:15:15,955 --> 00:15:18,776
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing unreasonable for you to say, thank you, if I give you food.

227
00:15:18,896 --> 00:15:19,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

228
00:15:19,616 --> 00:15:21,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Or to smile and say, oh, thank you, I really appreciate it.

229
00:15:22,177 --> 00:15:22,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that wrong?

230
00:15:22,817 --> 00:15:24,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But this monk wouldn't even look at me.

231
00:15:25,563 --> 00:15:35,387
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, even seemingly reasonable stuff, it's not reasonable if it's rooted in unreasonable expectations and views, basically craving and greed and aversion.

232
00:15:35,447 --> 00:15:36,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleases me, displeases me.

233
00:15:39,349 --> 00:15:45,852
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha had greater context whereby this type of behavior is more conducive for the future generations as well.

234
00:15:46,952 --> 00:15:50,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, those monks might have been able to handle associating with lay people.

235
00:15:51,261 --> 00:16:06,985
[SPEAKER_01]: But those monks, they will be monks who will come later on, who will not be able to handle it, but will see that as the way of doing things, associating, you know, let's get together, do festivals together, spend time together, sit in the groups together, go for walks together, but they will not be able to handle it.

236
00:16:08,446 --> 00:16:10,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the dhamma will erode as a result of it.

237
00:16:12,047 --> 00:16:15,928
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're able to handle it, then you're also able to not do it as well.

238
00:16:19,007 --> 00:16:28,387
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if those monks were able to handle lay people and surrounded by lay people, which some of them were, means they're also then able to handle not engaging with lay people.

239
00:16:29,081 --> 00:16:30,541
[SPEAKER_00]: With the greatest perspective.

240
00:16:30,561 --> 00:16:30,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

241
00:16:31,081 --> 00:16:32,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, they will be unaffected.

242
00:16:33,002 --> 00:16:37,463
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, sometimes you, circumstantially, can be involved with lay people.

243
00:16:37,863 --> 00:16:40,064
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the motivation behind that's going to determine it.

244
00:16:40,384 --> 00:16:53,707
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will know whether the motivation is good or bad, wholesome or unwholesome, if you review it and you see that, what if I'm not involved, would I be affected or emotionally... and then you realize, yes, means, okay, so I'm not impartial to this, I'm not indifferent to this.

245
00:16:54,487 --> 00:16:56,227
[SPEAKER_01]: If you truly don't mind, then it's fine.

246
00:16:56,567 --> 00:16:58,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Venerable Chana, who was a Narahant

247
00:16:59,614 --> 00:17:05,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And when Sariputta and Mahamoggalana went to see him, and they questioned him whether he's an arahant.

248
00:17:05,739 --> 00:17:07,540
[SPEAKER_01]: He said he is, but they didn't believe him.

249
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:11,503
[SPEAKER_01]: They went to the Buddha and said, oh, by the way, he died.

250
00:17:11,543 --> 00:17:15,386
[SPEAKER_01]: He committed... he used the knife because he was sick and so on.

251
00:17:16,227 --> 00:17:17,388
[SPEAKER_01]: What was his destination?

252
00:17:17,428 --> 00:17:19,769
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said, well, didn't he say he's an arahant?

253
00:17:20,290 --> 00:17:24,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And they said, well, he did say he was an arahant, but he was also associating with bad families.

254
00:17:25,558 --> 00:17:27,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there wasn't a good reputation going around.

255
00:17:28,559 --> 00:17:32,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, well, yes, but one is not blameworthy by associating with bad families.

256
00:17:32,621 --> 00:17:35,842
[SPEAKER_01]: One is blameworthy by leaving this body and taking up another.

257
00:17:36,022 --> 00:17:38,283
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, not being a Narahant, that's why you're blameworthy.

258
00:17:38,823 --> 00:17:46,386
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he tells Sariputta and Moggallāna, who are Arahants themselves, he said, I reassure you, Venerable Chānyu used that knife blamelessly.

259
00:17:46,727 --> 00:17:47,827
[SPEAKER_01]: He died blamelessly.

260
00:17:47,867 --> 00:17:48,607
[SPEAKER_01]: He died a Narahant.

261
00:17:48,647 --> 00:17:49,948
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a Narahant before he died.

262
00:17:52,511 --> 00:18:05,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize then, okay, so, although externally he was associating with his bad families, which would have not set a good example for other monks who would not be able to handle it, there was probably some practical or circumstantial reason that resulted in that.

263
00:18:05,539 --> 00:18:11,603
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't rooted in his greed, aversion, delusion, that made him then forsake the context and engage with bad families.

264
00:18:12,183 --> 00:18:13,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean he was doing bad stuff.

265
00:18:16,628 --> 00:18:19,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the motivation, the intention behind the action determines it.

266
00:18:20,192 --> 00:18:23,896
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot judge it simply because, oh, look, the way he walks means he's that.

267
00:18:24,757 --> 00:18:27,160
[SPEAKER_01]: The way he holds the cup means he's ignorant.

268
00:18:28,902 --> 00:18:31,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he chooses to do this, that means he's such and such.

269
00:18:32,266 --> 00:18:34,368
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's you projecting your own meaning into it.

270
00:18:35,690 --> 00:18:38,673
[SPEAKER_01]: And to that extent means you're unaware of your own meaning behind it.

